              "Expanding this anti-fibrotic paradigm, Zhou, Li, Zhou, Tan & Liu (Journal of the American Society of Nephrology 2013; doi:10.1681/ASN.2012080865) demonstrated that renal injury triggers rapid epigenetic and inflammatory loss of endogenous tubular α-Klotho, which unleashes hyperactive canonical Wnt/β-catenin signaling (Wnt1, Wnt4, Wnt7a) — a master driver of myofibroblast activation and extracellular matrix (fibronectin, collagen I/III) deposition. Soluble α-Klotho physically binds and sequesters multiple Wnt ligands in the extracellular space, preventing Wnt binding to Frizzled/LRP5/6 coreceptors, restoring active GSK-3β-mediated degradation of β-catenin, and suppressing target profibrotic genes (PAI-1, Snail1, MMP-7). Because TGF-β1, Wnt/β-catenin, and IGF-1/PI3K cross-amplify one another during chronic tissue injury, soluble α-Klotho's unique ability to simultaneously inhibit TGF-βR2, Wnt ligands, and IGF-1R explains why systemic Klotho delivery reverses established tubulointerstitial fibrosis and prevents uremic cardiomyopathy (where sKlotho also blocks PI3K-dependent exocytosis of transient receptor potential canonical 6 [TRPC6] calcium channels in cardiomyocytes).",
